{"uid":"90a739203adf609","name":"QA 2115","fullName":"QA #2115 on ubuntu2004","historyId":"2115@ubuntu2004","time":{"start":1795312466000,"stop":1795312467000,"duration":1000},"description":"PCP QA Test No. 2115\n\n\n\nCheck for memory errors when fetching derived metrics from an archive.\n\n\n\n__pmLogFetch() constructs a \"no values available\" pmValueSet by aliasing\n\na pmid_ctl struct over vset[j] (see the \"two tricks\" comment there).\n\nThat struct is only as big as the pmid and numval fields, so reading\n\nvset[j]->valfmt from one is 4 bytes off the end of the allocation.\n\n__dmpostvalueset() used to read valfmt unconditionally.\n\n\n\nCopyright (c) 2026 Red Hat.  All Rights Reserved.\n\n\n\n","descriptionHtml":"<p>PCP QA Test No. 2115</p>\n<p>Check for memory errors when fetching derived metrics from an archive.</p>\n<p>__pmLogFetch() constructs a &quot;no values available&quot; pmValueSet by aliasing</p>\n<p>a pmid_ctl struct over vset[j] (see the &quot;two tricks&quot; comment there).</p>\n<p>That struct is only as big as the pmid and numval fields, so reading</p>\n<p>vset[j]-&gt;valfmt from one is 4 bytes off the end of the allocation.</p>\n<p>__dmpostvalueset() used to read valfmt unconditionally.</p>\n<p>Copyright (c) 2026 Red Hat.  All Rights Reserved.</p>\n","status":"passed","statusMessage":"","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[],"afterStages":[],"labels":[{"name":"suite","value":"ubuntu2004"},{"name":"host","value":"ubuntu2004"},{"name":"epic","value":"derive"},{"name":"epic","value":"libpcp"},{"name":"epic","value":"valgrind"},{"name":"epic","value":"local"},{"name":"resultFormat","value":"allure2"}],"parameters":[],"links":[{"name":"Source","url":"https://github.com/performancecopilot/pcp/tree/c41b3724afcc28ebf7909148ffbdc1e1a2f5edaf/qa/2115","type":"test_case"},{"name":"Expected output","url":"https://github.com/performancecopilot/pcp/tree/c41b3724afc